t every vertex, although the displayed weighting has submodular capacity. This establishes the graph-level failure independently of merely observing negative curvature in one example. A third result establishes minimal Kron obstructions of unbounded order. Kron reduction eliminates internal vertices by taking a Schur complement of the Laplacian while preserving effective resistances between retained vertices. For every m ≥ 3, consider Kₘ₊₂ with one hub, spoke conductance 2m−1, and outer-edge conductance 2m−5. Its full capacity is not submodular, yet every proper Kron reduction onto at least two vertices is submodular. Every proper reduction retaining at least three vertices has strictly positive elementary gaps; normalized two-vertex capacity has an unavoidable equality. The proof uses the same scalar boundary. Eliminating outer vertices preserves the spoke-to-outer conductance ratio up to common scaling, whereas eliminating the hub produces a uniform complete graph. At the chosen ratio, Fₘ(t) &gt; 0 but Fₘ₊₁(t) &lt; 0. Thus all proper reductions pass while the full network fails. The smallest example is a five-vertex complete graph with spoke conductance 5 and outer-edge conductance 1; its four negative full-scale gaps each equal −5/192. It follows that no fixed retained-vertex bound can certify capacity submodularity by checking all Kron reductions up to that size on graphs of unrestricted order. The proofs are organized by an exact signed-curvature transport law. If T is retained and I is eliminated, then p^(T) = p_T − L_TI L_II⁻¹p_I, and the radius loss is r(V) − r(T) = 2p_IᵀL_II⁻¹p_I. These identities connect elimination, curvature redistribution, and capacity gaps. They yield a pairwise quadratic criterion explaining how opposite-sign reduced curvatures can still satisfy submodularity. Classical resistance geometry, Schur complements, and spanning-tree identities provide the underlying tools. The release also retains sharp minimality results: fixed-weight coun