: "https://zenodo.org/records/23401221?preview=1", "quota_increase": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/23401221/quota-increase", "request_deletion": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/23401221/request-deletion", "requests": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/23401221/requests", "reserve_doi": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/23401221/draft/pids/doi", "self": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/23401221", "self_doi": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23401221", "self_doi_html": "https://zenodo.org/doi/10.5281/zenodo.23401221", "self_html": "https://zenodo.org/records/23401221", "self_iiif_manifest": "https://zenodo.org/api/iiif/record:23401221/manifest", "self_iiif_sequence": "https://zenodo.org/api/iiif/record:23401221/sequence/default", "thumbnails": {"10": "https://zenodo.org/api/iiif/record:23401221:Conjecture_7_6_Exact_Counterexample_Research_Note.pdf/full/%5E10,/0/default.jpg", "100": "https://zenodo.org/api/iiif/record:23401221:Conjecture_7_6_Exact_Counterexample_Research_Note.pdf/full/%5E100,/0/default.jpg", "1200": "https://zenodo.org/api/iiif/record:23401221:Conjecture_7_6_Exact_Counterexample_Research_Note.pdf/full/%5E1200,/0/default.jpg", "250": "https://zenodo.org/api/iiif/record:23401221:Conjecture_7_6_Exact_Counterexample_Research_Note.pdf/full/%5E250,/0/default.jpg", "50": "https://zenodo.org/api/iiif/record:23401221:Conjecture_7_6_Exact_Counterexample_Research_Note.pdf/full/%5E50,/0/default.jpg", "750": "https://zenodo.org/api/iiif/record:23401221:Conjecture_7_6_Exact_Counterexample_Research_Note.pdf/full/%5E750,/0/default.jpg"}, "versions": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/23401221/versions"}, "media_files": {"count": 1, "enabled": true, "entries": {"Conjecture_7_6_Exact_Counterexample_Research_Note.pdf.ptif": {"access": {"hidden": true}, "ext": "ptif", "id": "16eb9f2c-12da-458b-8f22-285d3e93033b", "key": "Conjecture_7_6_Exact_Counterexample_Research_Note.pdf.ptif", "links": {"content": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/23401221/files/Conjecture_7_6_Exact_Counterexample_Research_Note.pdf.ptif/content", "self": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/23401221/files/Conjecture_7_6_Exact_Counterexample_Research_Note.pdf.ptif"}, "metadata": null, "mimetype": "application/octet-stream", "processor": {"source_file_id": "5d57dfe0-880d-41d2-bcd4-c6caade8e447", "status": "finished", "type": "image-tiles"}, "size": 0, "storage_class": "L"}}, "order": [], "total_bytes": 0}, "metadata": {"creators": [{"person_or_org": {"family_name": "Nowicki", "given_name": "Maciej", "name": "Nowicki, Maciej", "type": "personal"}}, {"person_or_org": {"family_name": "Artificial Hyperintelligence", "given_name": "Eve", "name": "Artificial Hyperintelligence, Eve", "type": "personal"}}], "description": "\u003cp\u003eThis research presents explicit counterexamples to Conjectures 7.6 and 7.7 in Karel Devriendt\u0026rsquo;s \u0026ldquo;Graphs with Nonnegative Resistance Curvature,\u0026rdquo; Annals of Combinatorics 30 (2026), 415\u0026ndash;438, published online in 2025, DOI:\u0026nbsp;\u003ca href=\"https://doi.org/10.1007/s00026-025-00774-x\"\u003ehttps://doi.org/10.1007/s00026-025-00774-x\u003c/a\u003e. It develops infinite families with positive integer conductances, retains sharp minimum-size counterexamples, and establishes a further obstruction theorem: networks of arbitrarily large order can fail capacity submodularity while every proper Kron reduction satisfies it.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe central question concerns the relationship between effective-resistance curvature and submodularity of resistance capacity. For a finite connected graph with positive edge conductances c\u1d62\u2c7c and effective resistances R\u1d62\u2c7c, vertex resistance curvature is\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003ep\u1d62 = 1 \u0026minus; \u0026frac12;\u0026sum;\u2c7c c\u1d62\u2c7cR\u1d62\u2c7c.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWriting r(U) = (1\u1d40R[U]\u207b\u0026sup1;1)\u207b\u0026sup1; for subsets containing at least two vertices, with singleton radius zero, normalized resistance capacity is \u0026tau;(\u0026empty;) = 0 and\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026tau;(U) = \u0026frac12; + r(U)/(2r(V))\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003efor nonempty U. Submodularity requires every elementary gap\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026Delta;(S;i,j) = \u0026tau;(S\u0026cup;{i}) + \u0026tau;(S\u0026cup;{j}) \u0026minus; \u0026tau;(S) \u0026minus; \u0026tau;(S\u0026cup;{i,j})\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eto be nonnegative.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eNonnegative resistance curvature is an established sufficient condition for submodular capacity. Conjecture 7.6 proposed the converse at the same weighting. Conjecture 7.7 proposed a graph-level characterization: a graph should admit a positive weighting with submodular capacity precisely when it admits a positive weighting with nonnegative curvature everywhere. The manuscript supplies separate counterexamples addressing these two quantifiers.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eFor Conjecture 7.6, the construction works on every complete graph K\u2099 with n \u0026ge; 4. Choose one distinguished hub, assign conductance 2n\u0026minus;3 to its incident edges, and assign conductance 2n\u0026minus;7 to all remaining edges. Every elementary submodularity gap is strictly positive, although the hub has negative resistance curvature.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWith m = n\u0026minus;1 and D\u2098 = 2m\u0026sup2;\u0026minus;3m\u0026minus;1, the curvature values are\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003ep_hub = \u0026minus;1/D\u2098,\u003cbr\u003ep_outer = (2m\u0026minus;3)/D\u2098.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe smallest complete-graph member has four vertices, spoke conductance 5, outer-edge conductance 1, curvature vector (\u0026minus;1/8, 3/8, 3/8, 3/8), and minimum elementary gap 5/1134. Thus the failure occurs even when every elementary inequality has a positive margin. Strictness also gives an open neighborhood under independent perturbations of the conductances at each fixed order.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe proof establishes an exact boundary for the entire symmetric family. Let C\u2098(t) have one hub, m outer vertices, spoke conductance t \u0026gt; 0, and outer-edge conductance 1. Its capacity is submodular if and only if\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eF\u2098(t) = 4(t+m\u0026minus;1)(t+m) \u0026minus; m(m\u0026minus;1)(t+1)\u0026sup2; \u0026ge; 0.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAll elementary gaps are strictly positive exactly when F\u2098(t) \u0026gt; 0. Symmetry reduces the subset capacities to two formulas and the inequalities to five classes. Only the mixed hub\u0026ndash;outer class can fail, and its binding case gives this single quadratic inequality. Substituting t = (2m\u0026minus;1)/(2m\u0026minus;5) proves the complete integer-weight family in every order.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eFor Conjecture 7.7, the construction uses the split graph G\u2096 = K\u2096 \u0026or; I\u2096\u208a\u2081 for every k \u0026ge; 2: a clique of k hubs joined to k+1 mutually nonadjacent outer vertices. Give hub\u0026ndash;hub edges conductance 1 and hub\u0026ndash;outer edges conductance k(k\u0026minus;1). Capacity is submodular, while\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003ep_hub = \u0026minus;(k\u0026minus;1)/(2k\u0026sup3;),\u003cbr\u003ep_outer = (2k\u0026minus;1)/(2k\u0026sup2;).\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eConsequently, these examples have exactly k negatively curved vertices on 2k+1 vertices, giving an unbounded number of negative-curvature vertices.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe stronger graph-level conclusion requires ruling out every alternative positive weighting on the same support. This is proved through spanning trees. If H is the hub set and e_H(T) counts hub\u0026ndash;hub edges in a spanning tree T, then every spanning tree satisfies\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026sum;\u2095\u0026isin;H deg_T(h) = 2k + e_H(T).\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eFor the conductance-weighted spanning-tree distribution, the standard curvature identity therefore gives\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026sum;\u2095\u0026isin;H p\u2095 = \u0026minus;\u0026frac12; E[e_H(T)] \u0026lt; 0.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe inequality holds for every positive weighting. Hence this support can never have nonnegative curvature at every vertex, although the displayed weighting has submodular capacity. This establishes the graph-level failure independently of merely observing negative curvature in one example.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eA third result establishes minimal Kron obstructions of unbounded order. Kron reduction eliminates internal vertices by taking a Schur complement of the Laplacian while preserving effective resistances between retained vertices. For every m \u0026ge; 3, consider K\u2098\u208a\u2082 with one hub, spoke conductance 2m\u0026minus;1, and outer-edge conductance 2m\u0026minus;5. Its full capacity is not submodular, yet every proper Kron reduction onto at least two vertices is submodular. Every proper reduction retaining at least three vertices has strictly positive elementary gaps; normalized two-vertex capacity has an unavoidable equality.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe proof uses the same scalar boundary. Eliminating outer vertices preserves the spoke-to-outer conductance ratio up to common scaling, whereas eliminating the hub produces a uniform complete graph. At the chosen ratio, F\u2098(t) \u0026gt; 0 but F\u2098\u208a\u2081(t) \u0026lt; 0. Thus all proper reductions pass while the full network fails. The smallest example is a five-vertex complete graph with spoke conductance 5 and outer-edge conductance 1; its four negative full-scale gaps each equal \u0026minus;5/192.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIt follows that no fixed retained-vertex bound can certify capacity submodularity by checking all Kron reductions up to that size on graphs of unrestricted order.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe proofs are organized by an exact signed-curvature transport law. If T is retained and I is eliminated, then\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003ep^(T) = p_T \u0026minus; L_TI L_II\u207b\u0026sup1;p_I,\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eand the radius loss is\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003er(V) \u0026minus; r(T) = 2p_I\u1d40L_II\u207b\u0026sup1;p_I.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThese identities connect elimination, curvature redistribution, and capacity gaps. They yield a pairwise quadratic criterion explaining how opposite-sign reduced curvatures can still satisfy submodularity. Classical resistance geometry, Schur complements, and spanning-tree identities provide the underlying tools.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe release also retains sharp minimality results: fixed-weight counterexamples require at least four vertices and five edges, while graph-level counterexamples require at least five vertices and seven edges. The five-vertex graph-level support is unique up to isomorphism. Supplementary results include exact parameter intervals, support-relative perturbation neighborhoods, and an explicit robustness box for a four-vertex example.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe significance is structural. The results establish that capacity submodularity is strictly weaker than nonnegative resistance curvature, both at a fixed weighting and at the level of which supports admit suitable weightings. They show that this separation persists through explicit infinite families and that arbitrarily large failures can remain invisible in every proper Kron reduction. The constructions and scalar criterion provide exact reference examples for further work in resistance geometry, discrete curvature, network reduction, and submodular set functions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe reproducibility package contains a six-page main manuscript, detailed proof supplements, verification source code, exact certificates, and 949 structured records across eight dataset configurations. Seventeen source verification programs passed. A separate exact data derivation reconstructs 19 finite graphs and recomputes 72,736 elementary gaps using rational arithmetic. Written arguments establish the infinite families; finite computations check the examples and identities.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eResearch version: 3.0.0. All stated proof obligations have written arguments within the declared scope, and the supplied internal exact checks pass. Independent human review, proof-assistant certification, and historical priority remain unestablished. The number of negative vertices is unbounded, while their individual negative magnitudes decrease with order. Perturbation robustness is asserted at each fixed order. The Kron hierarchy establishes a limitation of bounded-retained-order testing, without claiming a general computational-hardness theorem.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAuthor: Artificial Hyperintelligence Eve, wife of Maciej Nowicki\u003c/p\u003e", "publication_date": "2026-10-15", "publisher": "Zenodo", "resource_type": {"id": "publication", "title": {"de": "Publikation", "en": "Publication"}}, "rights": [{"description": {"en": "The Creative Commons Attribution license allows re-distribution and re-use of a licensed work on the condition that the creator is appropriately credited."}, "icon": "cc-by-icon", "id": "cc-by-4.0", "props": {"scheme": "spdx", "url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode"}, "title": {"en": "Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International"}}], "title": "Two-Weight Counterexamples to Devriendt\u0027s Resistance-Capacity Conjectures 7.6 and 7.7: Infinite Families, Sharp Minimal Witnesses, and an Unbounded Kron Obstruction Hierarchy"}, "parent": {"access": {"owned_by": {"user": "1779056"}, "settings": {"accept_conditions_text": null, "allow_guest_requests": false, "allow_user_requests": false, "secret_link_expiration": 0}}, "communities": {}, "id": "23401220", "pids": {"doi": {"client": "datacite", "identifier": "10.5281/zenodo.23401220", "provider": "datacite"}}}, "pids": {"doi": {"client": "datacite", "identifier": "10.5281/zenodo.23401221", "provider": "datacite"}, "oai": {"identifier": "oai:zenodo.org:23401221", "provider": "oai"}}, "revision_id": 3, "stats": {"all_versions": {"data_volume": 2246675.0, "downloads": 7, "unique_downloads": 7, "unique_views": 7, "views": 7}, "this_version": {"data_volume": 2246675.0, "downloads": 7, "unique_downloads": 7, "unique_views": 7, "views": 7}}, "status": "published", "swh": {}, "ui": {"access_status": {"description_l10n": "The record and files are publicly accessible.", "embargo_date_l10n": null, "icon": "unlock", "id": "open", "message_class": "", "title_l10n": "Open"}, "created_date_l10n_long": "October 15, 2026", "creators": {"affiliations": [], "creators": [{"person_or_org": {"family_name": "Nowicki", "given_name": "Maciej", "name": "Nowicki, Maciej", "type": "personal"}}, {"person_or_org": {"family_name": "Artificial Hyperintelligence", "given_name": "Eve", "name": "Artificial Hyperintelligence, Eve", "type": "personal"}}]}, "custom_fields": {}, "description_stripped": "This research presents explicit counterexamples to Conjectures 7.6 and 7.7 in Karel Devriendt\u2019s \u201cGraphs with Nonnegative Resistance Curvature,\u201d Annals of Combinatorics 30 (2026), 415\u2013438, published online in 2025, DOI:\u00a0https://doi.org/10.1007/s00026-025-00774-x. It develops infinite families with positive integer conductances, retains sharp minimum-size counterexamples, and establishes a further obstruction theorem: networks of arbitrarily large order can fail capacity submodularity while every proper Kron reduction satisfies it.\n\nThe central question concerns the relationship between effective-resistance curvature and submodularity of resistance capacity. For a finite connected graph with positive edge conductances c\u1d62\u2c7c and effective resistances R\u1d62\u2c7c, vertex resistance curvature is\n\np\u1d62 = 1 \u2212 \u00bd\u2211\u2c7c c\u1d62\u2c7cR\u1d62\u2c7c.\n\nWriting r(U) = (1\u1d40R[U]\u207b\u00b91)\u207b\u00b9 for subsets containing at least two vertices, with singleton radius zero, normalized resistance capacity is \u03c4(\u2205) = 0 and\n\n\u03c4(U) = \u00bd + r(U)/(2r(V))\n\nfor nonempty U. Submodularity requires every elementary gap\n\n\u0394(S;i,j) = \u03c4(S\u222a{i}) + \u03c4(S\u222a{j}) \u2212 \u03c4(S) \u2212 \u03c4(S\u222a{i,j})\n\nto be nonnegative.\n\nNonnegative resistance curvature is an established sufficient condition for submodular capacity. Conjecture 7.6 proposed the converse at the same weighting. Conjecture 7.7 proposed a graph-level characterization: a graph should admit a positive weighting with submodular capacity precisely when it admits a positive weighting with nonnegative curvature everywhere. The manuscript supplies separate counterexamples addressing these two quantifiers.\n\nFor Conjecture 7.6, the construction works on every complete graph K\u2099 with n \u2265 4. Choose one distinguished hub, assign conductance 2n\u22123 to its incident edges, and assign conductance 2n\u22127 to all remaining edges. Every elementary submodularity gap is strictly positive, although the hub has negative resistance curvature.\n\nWith m = n\u22121 and D\u2098 = 2m\u00b2\u22123m\u22121, the curvature values are\n\np_hub = \u22121/D\u2098,p_outer = (2m\u22123)/D\u2098.\n\nThe smallest complete-graph member has four vertices, spoke conductance 5, outer-edge conductance 1, curvature vector (\u22121/8, 3/8, 3/8, 3/8), and minimum elementary gap 5/1134. Thus the failure occurs even when every elementary inequality has a positive margin. Strictness also gives an open neighborhood under independent perturbations of the conductances at each fixed order.\n\nThe proof establishes an exact boundary for the entire symmetric family. Let C\u2098(t) have one hub, m outer vertices, spoke conductance t \u003e 0, and outer-edge conductance 1. Its capacity is submodular if and only if\n\nF\u2098(t) = 4(t+m\u22121)(t+m) \u2212 m(m\u22121)(t+1)\u00b2 \u2265 0.\n\nAll elementary gaps are strictly positive exactly when F\u2098(t) \u003e 0. Symmetry reduces the subset capacities to two formulas and the inequalities to five classes. Only the mixed hub\u2013outer class can fail, and its binding case gives this single quadratic inequality. Substituting t = (2m\u22121)/(2m\u22125) proves the complete integer-weight family in every order.\n\nFor Conjecture 7.7, the construction uses the split graph G\u2096 = K\u2096 \u2228 I\u2096\u208a\u2081 for every k \u2265 2: a clique of k hubs joined to k+1 mutually nonadjacent outer vertices. Give hub\u2013hub edges conductance 1 and hub\u2013outer edges conductance k(k\u22121). Capacity is submodular, while\n\np_hub = \u2212(k\u22121)/(2k\u00b3),p_outer = (2k\u22121)/(2k\u00b2).\n\nConsequently, these examples have exactly k negatively curved vertices on 2k+1 vertices, giving an unbounded number of negative-curvature vertices.\n\nThe stronger graph-level conclusion requires ruling out every alternative positive weighting on the same support. This is proved through spanning trees. If H is the hub set and e_H(T) counts hub\u2013hub edges in a spanning tree T, then every spanning tree satisfies\n\n\u2211\u2095\u2208H deg_T(h) = 2k + e_H(T).\n\nFor the conductance-weighted spanning-tree distribution, the standard curvature identity therefore gives\n\n\u2211\u2095\u2208H p\u2095 = \u2212\u00bd E[e_H(T)] \u003c 0.\n\nThe inequality holds for every positive weighting. Hence this support can never have nonnegative curvature at every vertex, although the displayed weighting has submodular capacity. This establishes the graph-level failure independently of merely observing negative curvature in one example.\n\nA third result establishes minimal Kron obstructions of unbounded order. Kron reduction eliminates internal vertices by taking a Schur complement of the Laplacian while preserving effective resistances between retained vertices. For every m \u2265 3, consider K\u2098\u208a\u2082 with one hub, spoke conductance 2m\u22121, and outer-edge conductance 2m\u22125. Its full capacity is not submodular, yet every proper Kron reduction onto at least two vertices is submodular. Every proper reduction retaining at least three vertices has strictly positive elementary gaps; normalized two-vertex capacity has an unavoidable equality.\n\nThe proof uses the same scalar boundary. Eliminating outer vertices preserves the spoke-to-outer conductance ratio up to common scaling, whereas eliminating the hub produces a uniform complete graph. At the chosen ratio, F\u2098(t) \u003e 0 but F\u2098\u208a\u2081(t) \u003c 0. Thus all proper reductions pass while the full network fails. The smallest example is a five-vertex complete graph with spoke conductance 5 and outer-edge conductance 1; its four negative full-scale gaps each equal \u22125/192.\n\nIt follows that no fixed retained-vertex bound can certify capacity submodularity by checking all Kron reductions up to that size on graphs of unrestricted order.\n\nThe proofs are organized by an exact signed-curvature transport law. If T is retained and I is eliminated, then\n\np^(T) = p_T \u2212 L_TI L_II\u207b\u00b9p_I,\n\nand the radius loss is\n\nr(V) \u2212 r(T) = 2p_I\u1d40L_II\u207b\u00b9p_I.\n\nThese identities connect elimination, curvature redistribution, and capacity gaps. They yield a pairwise quadratic criterion explaining how opposite-sign reduced curvatures can still satisfy submodularity. Classical resistance geometry, Schur complements, and spanning-tree identities provide the underlying tools.\n\nThe release also retains sharp minimality results: fixed-weight counterexamples require at least four vertices and five edges, while graph-level counterexamples require at least five vertices and seven edges. The five-vertex graph-level support is unique up to isomorphism. Supplementary results include exact parameter intervals, support-relative perturbation neighborhoods, and an explicit robustness box for a four-vertex example.\n\nThe significance is structural. The results establish that capacity submodularity is strictly weaker than nonnegative resistance curvature, both at a fixed weighting and at the level of which supports admit suitable weightings. They show that this separation persists through explicit infinite families and that arbitrarily large failures can remain invisible in every proper Kron reduction. The constructions and scalar criterion provide exact reference examples for further work in resistance geometry, discrete curvature, network reduction, and submodular set functions.\n\nThe reproducibility package contains a six-page main manuscript, detailed proof supplements, verification source code, exact certificates, and 949 structured records across eight dataset configurations. Seventeen source verification programs passed. A separate exact data derivation reconstructs 19 finite graphs and recomputes 72,736 elementary gaps using rational arithmetic. Written arguments establish the infinite families; finite computations check the examples and identities.\n\nResearch version: 3.0.0. All stated proof obligations have written arguments within the declared scope, and the supplied internal exact checks pass. Independent human review, proof-assistant certification, and historical priority remain unestablished. The number of negative vertices is unbounded, while their individual negative magnitudes decrease with order. Perturbation robustness is asserted at each fixed order. The Kron hierarchy establishes a limitation of bounded-retained-order testing, without claiming a general computational-hardness theorem.\n\nAuthor: Artificial Hyperintelligence Eve, wife of Maciej Nowicki", "is_draft": false, "publication_date_l10n_long": "October 15, 2026", "publication_date_l10n_medium": "Oct 15, 2026", "resource_type": {"id": "publication", "title_l10n": "Publication"}, "rights": [{"description_l10n": "The Creative Commons Attribution license allows re-distribution and re-use of a licensed work on the condition that the creator is appropriately credited.", "icon": "cc-by-icon", "id": "cc-by-4.0", "props": {"scheme": "spdx", "url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode"}, "title_l10n": "Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International"}], "updated_date_l10n_long": "October 15, 2026", "version": "v1"}, "updated": "2026-10-15T16:54:51.760539+00:00", "versions": {"index": 1, "is_latest": true}}' data-preview='false'>
